<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n1177" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Aegĭpān</span></b> ,
		ānis, or Gr. ānos (<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>dat. plur.</i>
			Aegipanis, <i><span class="en">Mart. Cap. 6, p. 215</span></i>),
				m., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Αἰγίπαν</span>.
			</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Goat-Pan</i>, i. e. <i>goat-shaped
			Pan</i>, <i>a well-known sylvan deity with goat's feet
				and rough body</i>, <i><span class="en">Hyg.
				Astr. 2, 28</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> Acc. to <i><span class="en">Mel. 1, 4, 10</span></i>; <i><span class="en">1, 8, 10</span></i>; and <i><span class="en">Plin. 5, 8,
				8, § 46</span></i>, <i>a kind of goat-shaped
				men in Africa</i>, perh. <i>the
		baboon.</i></div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
